02AC 39               (1/modules/rbf.asm):00491                             rts
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00492         
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00493         
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00494         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00495         * I$ChgDir Entry Point
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00496         *    The user's requested mode is in PD.MOD,y.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00497         *    It should have at least one bit of READ. or WRITE. or EXEC.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00498         *    CWD is changed if READ. ($1) or WRITE. ($2) is set.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00499         *    CXD is changed if EXEC. ($4) is set.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00500         *    Both may be changed.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00501         *    If no bits were set, it does nothing but returns OK.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00502         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00503         *    (Level 1 used to allow PEXEC. ($20) as well as EXEC. ($4),
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00504         *    but Level 2 did not.  To unify the levels, now both will
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00505         *    accept PEXEC., but this was probably never documented.)
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00506         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00507         *    IOMan creates a temporary 64-byte Path Descriptor,
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00508         *    and closes the descriptor before IOMan returns.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00509         *    That is the path descriptor passed to ChgDir in the Y register.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00510         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00511         *    Using that path descriptor, IOMan calls I$Attach on the device before
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00512         *    calling the ChgDir entry here, and calls I$Detach on the device after
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00513         *    we return.  I$Attach links the Device Descriptor module, its Driver
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00514         *    module, and its File Mananger module.  I$Detach unlinks all three.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00515         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00516         *    Here's how this seems to work.  In the process descriptor,
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00517         *    there are 12 bytes, starting with the label P$DIO.  The headers
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00518         *    do not describe the internal structure.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00519         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00520         *    IOMan imposes this much structure:
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00521         *    The Device Table Entry Pointer for the CWD device
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00522         *    is two bytes stored at offset 0.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00523         *    The Device Table Entry Pointer for the CXD device
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00524         *    is two bytes stored at offset 6.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00525         *    That implies that the File Manager of a device with directories
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00526         *    can use four bytes, offsets 2 through 5, to remember which directory
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00527         *    is the CWD; and can use four bytes, offsets 8 through 11,
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00528         *    to remember which directory is the CXD.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00529         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00530         *    RBF saves the 3-byte PSN Sector Number of the inode of the CWD
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00531         *    in offsets 3 through 5.  It appears offset 2 is unused.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00532         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00533         *    RBF saves the 3-byte PSN Sector Number of the inode of the CXD
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00534         *    in offsets 9 through 11.  It appears offset 8 is unused.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00535         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00536         *    Is there any Link Count on the Device (in the Device Table Entry)
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00537         *    to mark the fact that a process is using it for its CWD or CXD?
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00538         *    It seems to me (strick) that there should be, but I cannot find
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00539         *    where a link count is being maintained.  This should involve
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00540         *    three things:  1. A new process inherits CWD and CXD from its
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00541         *    parent process, so it should add a link to each device;
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00542         *    2.  When a process changes a current directory, it should unlink
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00543         *    the old one, and link the new one;  and 3.  A process unlinks them
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00544         *    when the process dies.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00545         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00546         *    However I think in Nitros9 actually 1. A new process gets a copy
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00547         *    of its parent's 12 byte P$DIO structure, but no links are done;
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00548         *    2. When one changes directories, the old values are ignored
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00549         *    and the new values are not linked; and 3. When a process dies,
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00550         *    its P$DIO structure is ignored, and just vanishes.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00551         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00552         *    (In most unices, the CWD and CRD (working and root) directories
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00553         *    are ordinary open paths to the directory, which would link the
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00554         *    usage of the device.  Why didn't OS9 do the same thing?)
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00555         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00556         *    Fact: The name `P$DIO` does not occur in any of the Level2
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00557         *    kernel module sources.   In Level1, it only appears in `ffork.asm`
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00558         *    where it is used to copy the 12 bytes from parent to child.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00559         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00560         *    Bug?  If you unlink a device (with no open paths) while some
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00561         *    process uses it for CWD or CXD, are not the Device Table
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00562         *    Entry Pointers now pointing to garbage?
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00563         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00564         * Entry:
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00565         *    Y -> a temporary path descriptor, created just for this operation.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00566         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00567         * How this block exits:
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00568         *    The entry Y value should have been pushed onto the stack.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00569         *    Notice all exits are branches to Clos2A0.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00570         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00571         * Exit:
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00572         *    The only result is a side effect in the P$DIO structure in the
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00573         *    process descriptor.
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00574         *
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00575         * Error: CC Carry set
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00576         *        B = errcode
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00577         *
02AD 3420             (1/modules/rbf.asm):00578         ChgDir              pshs      y                   preserve path descriptor pointer (to be pulled at Clos2A0)
                      (1/modules/rbf.asm):00579                             ifne      UNTESTED_H6309
